Chia timelord on macOS — Full Disk Access
==========================================

Relevant only for topology local-external. ~/.chia is a real directory on the
internal disk, and CHIA_ROOT ({{ chia_root }}) stays there, including the wallet
database. Only {{ chia_root }}/db is a symlink to the on-volume database
directory ({{ chia_darwin_volume_db_dir }}) on {{ chia_darwin_db_volume }}.
local-internal and remote-node keep everything on the internal disk and do not
need this grant.

Why
---
LaunchDaemons run outside a user GUI session and have no Transparency, Consent,
and Control (TCC) grants of their own. A job whose program is a script is
attributed to its shebang interpreter. These wrappers use
{{ chia_darwin_interpreter }}, so that one grant is shared by every wrapped job.

Only the full-node wrapper waits on the volume. It lists
{{ chia_darwin_db_marker }} with /bin/ls, a readdir operation. Other wrappers do
not wait on the volume. When Full Disk Access is missing, the full node can see
that {{ chia_darwin_db_volume }} is mounted and still fail to read
{{ chia_darwin_volume_db_dir }}. The wrapper then loops on "waiting for
{{ chia_darwin_db_volume }}" and the log's ls= line is "Operation not permitted".
An interactive SSH session can read the same path while launchd cannot. That
mismatch is TCC, not a missing mount.

Full Disk Access is needed only for:
1. {{ chia_darwin_interpreter }} — launchd uses it as the shebang interpreter for
   every job wrapper, so this grant is shared by every wrapped job;
2. the full node's Python interpreter — the real Homebrew binary that the venv
   python3.13 resolves to:

   {{ chia_darwin_fda_python }}

That Python grant is tied to the Cellar version path. A Homebrew Python upgrade
changes the path, and the node loses DB access until the grant is added for the
new binary.

Do not add companion binaries, tailscaled, alloy, exporters, hw-vdf, the
watchdog, Bash, or healthcheck to Full Disk Access for this layout. Companion
jobs keep their CHIA_ROOT on the internal disk and do not wait for the database
volume.

How to grant it
---------------
1. GUI (this Mac):
   System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Full Disk Access -> add
   {{ chia_darwin_interpreter }} and {{ chia_darwin_fda_python }}
   (Cmd-Shift-G if either is hidden) and enable both.
   Then restart the full-node LaunchDaemon
   (launchctl kickstart -k system/<full-node label>).

2. MDM: install the sibling profile
   {{ chia_darwin_pppc_dest }}
   through user-approved MDM or Automated Device Enrollment.
   The profile grants only {{ chia_darwin_interpreter }}. It does not grant the
   full node's Python interpreter. Add {{ chia_darwin_fda_python }} by hand in
   System Settings (step 1), and again after every Homebrew Python upgrade.
   Opening the .mobileconfig locally does not grant SystemPolicyAllFiles
   on modern macOS.

Do not edit TCC.db. It is SIP-protected, and writing it does not grant
Full Disk Access to launchd jobs.

Scope
-----
The {{ chia_darwin_interpreter }} grant uses the identifier path and code
requirement {{ chia_darwin_pppc_code_requirement }}. Any other LaunchDaemon that
starts through that interpreter can then read protected files, including
external volumes. That is the interpreter the running deployment uses. This role
will not swap in a different interpreter or a custom Mach-O stub.

The Python path in this note is resolved from the venv when the role writes the
file. The PPPC profile template keeps only the /bin/zsh identifier because a
versioned Cellar path goes stale on the next Python upgrade.

This role cannot grant TCC Full Disk Access. It only writes this note and the
profile template. It never runs `profiles`, `tccutil`, or SQL against TCC.db.
